# Navigation & deep linking

**Primary source:** Jetpack Navigation Compose with a typed route,
Android App Links (`autoVerify` intent filters), and App
Actions/App Shortcuts for launcher/Assistant entry points — all
Google-native APIs. No third-party layer is needed here.

## Typed route model

Every flow's navigable destinations are a `Serializable`/`@Serializable`
sealed type — not raw string routes with manually interpolated
arguments:

```kotlin
@Serializable
sealed interface AppRoute {
    @Serializable data object Login : AppRoute
    @Serializable data object SystemStatus : AppRoute
}
```

- One `NavHost` for the app, driven by the typed `AppRoute` sealed
  type — never a raw string route built with manual string
  concatenation for anything beyond the simplest static case.
- Navigating means calling `navController.navigate(AppRoute.SystemStatus)`
  — type-checked at compile time, not a string that can typo at runtime.

## App Links (Android's Universal Links equivalent)

- Declare `autoVerify="true"` intent filters in the manifest for the
  app's Android App Links domain; **verify** the Digital Asset Links
  file is correctly hosted — an unverified App Link silently falls back
  to a disambiguation dialog instead of opening the app directly.
- Resolve incoming deep link URIs into an `AppRoute` in **one place**
  (the `NavHost`'s deep link handling, or a dedicated resolver called
  from there) — not per-feature URI parsing scattered across the
  codebase.
- Test both "cold start with a pending deep link" and "already running,
  link arrives while foregrounded" — not just the happy warm-start case.
- If the deep link targets an authenticated screen and the user isn't
  logged in, route to `Login` first and replay the original destination
  after successful auth — do not silently drop the deep link.

## App Actions / Shortcuts

- Expose key actions (e.g. "check system status") as a static or dynamic
  App Shortcut (`ShortcutManagerCompat`) so they're reachable from the
  launcher's long-press menu without a separate integration per surface.
- An App Action's/Shortcut's target resolves to the same typed
  `AppRoute` the UI uses — it does not duplicate business logic.
- Only expose shortcuts for actions that make sense without the app's
  full UI context — not every screen needs one.

## Testing

- Route-resolution logic (URI → `AppRoute`) is pure and unit-testable
  without an emulator.
- The critical "cold start with a pending deep link" path gets one
  instrumented test using an explicit launch `Intent` with the deep link
  URI.

## Anti-patterns

- **String-keyed navigation** (`navController.navigate("detail/$id")`)
  parsed back out of a string, instead of a typed, serializable route.
- **Deep link parsing duplicated per feature** instead of one resolver.
- **An App Shortcut/Action that reimplements a service call** instead of
  delegating to the same `core` service the UI uses.

